the 8 month long round-the-world race  has reached the finish line and with it  an end to the most comprehensive  research yet on how much plastic is in  the world's oceans samples collected  throughout the 45 thousand mile journey  by the yacht turn the tide on plastic  are still being analyzed but they  revealed traces of micro plastic almost  everywhere including some of the most  remote parts of the planet the  Portuguese Mir Puri foundation like sky  ocean rescue one of the sponsors of the  mission says the findings are  devastating it will be catastrophic not  now but in the third generation each  time more we have this disease new  diseases from new contaminants we are  having more cancer more allergic  diseases more infertility we are less  fertile than our grandfathers are you  saying the plastic is killing the human  race yes is killing human race but  slowly we don't realize how many  chemicals we have in the world that  could interfere with us you just scare  mongering aren't you the way you use  plastic and this is so convenient for  modern life we are not gonna change if  you are not scary other scientists are  more measured in their conclusions but  share the same concerns because micro  plastic is consumed by shellfish which  are then eaten by humans we actually  don't know the extent of the risk at the  moment but I think we can say with some  certainty that if we don't both  understand the problem understand the  scientific challenges there will come a  point whether it be so much plastic in  the ocean that we will be facing hazards  that could be endangering human life  the crew have conducted the research  while at the same time battling  treacherous seas in a highly competitive  race having endured such dangerous  conditions they hope the findings will  indeed turn the tide on pasta I'm proud  to be part of it it gives you a great  sense of achievement and especially when  we get the results back I'm saddened by  the results but at the same time it's  because of us that they have them and  that's that's a great achievement the  event ends in the hague this week but  this research could be a watershed  moment in efforts to clean up the  world's oceans in Woods Sky News
 

